ear and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\",\n          \"Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 115,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 121,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 122,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.\",\n          \"Along with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s gross margin rose to 75% from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased. NVIDIA’s GAAP operating income has more than doubled year over year, implying significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 139,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 177,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs.\",\n          \"Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 232,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"c\",\n     \"value\": \"75% non-GAAP gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal second quarter of 2027 / Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA, reported\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-016\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin: **75%** | stored from 2 articles, each with its own details: || article 35: basis: Non-GAAP; versus 72.5% in the prior-year quarter | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Fiscal second quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 || article 37: basis: non-GAAP; from 72.5% | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 | said: 2026-08-26\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 35,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 37,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n          \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\",\n          \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"c\",\n     \"value\": \"75% gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter / last quarter / Reported quarter (not named in the item)\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA; NVIDIA Corporation, reported\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-034\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin: **75%** | basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal second quarter | said: not stored | article 55\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 55,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year.\",\n          \"The company also produced a 75% gross margin\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-039\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > last quarter\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin: **75%** | basis: company gross margin, as reported | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: last quarter | said: not stored | article 16\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 16,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-058\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > reported quarter (not named in the item)\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin: **75%** | basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA Corporation | period as stored: Reported quarter (not named in the item) | said: not stored | article 74\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 74,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) generated $96.2 billion of quarterly revenue, up 106%, with Data Center revenue rising 117% to $89.0 billion and gross margin at 75%.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ],\n   \"S4\": [\n    {\n     \"group\": \"a\",\n     \"value\": \"Gross margin 72% to 73% (company guidance, lowered on higher-than-expected memory costs; helped by price increases taking effect in fiscal Q1 2028)\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA, its own outlook (guidance section; source as stored NVIDIA management / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) / NVIDIA; said late August 2026)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-092\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2028\",\n       \"line\": \"Gross margin: **72% to 73%** | stored from 3 articles, each with its own details: || article 98: basis: Company gross margin guidance, lowered on higher-than-expected memory costs and helped by price increases already executed | source as stored: NVIDIA management | period as stored: Fiscal 2028 | said: not stored || article 121: basis: Expected level as price hikes take effect | source as stored: NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) | period as stored: Fiscal 2028 | said: late August 2026 || article 177: basis: management expectation, helped by price increases taking effect in fiscal Q1 2028 | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal 2028 | said: not stored\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 98,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 121,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 177,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA has already set price increases that take effect in fiscal Q1 2028. Management expects those higher prices to lift margin to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"a\",\n     \"value\": \"Gross margin 71% to 72% (management expects this to be the low point or trough, caused by higher memory prices)\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027 ('Fiscal 2027 fourth quarter' in T-091)\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA, its own outlook (guidance section; source as stored NVIDIA / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress); said August 2026 / late August 2026)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-090\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2027-Q4\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin: **71% to 72%** | stored from 2 articles, each with its own details: || article 115: basis: expected bottom | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal Q4 2027 | said: not stored || article 177: basis: management expects this to be the low point, caused by higher memory prices | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal Q4 2027 | said: August 2026\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 115,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 177,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs.\",\n          \"Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-091\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2027-Q4\",\n       \"line\": \"Gross margin: **between 71% and 72%** | basis: Expected trough; company outlook stated by CFO | source as stored: NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) | period as stored: Fiscal 2027 fourth quarter | said: late August 2026 | article 121\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 121,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"a\",\n     \"value\": \"Gross margin guidance approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points (GAAP and non-GAAP; low end 73.5%; down from fiscal Q2 2027; management cited higher component costs, especially memory)\",\n     \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027 (also stored as 'fiscal Q3 2027', 'fiscal third quarter of 2027')\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA, its own outlook (guidance section; source as stored NVIDIA / NVIDIA Corp. / NVIDIA Corporation; said 2026-08-26)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-077\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2027-Q3\",\n       \"line\": \"GAAP and non-GAAP gross margin: **approximately 74%** | basis: plus or minus 50 basis points | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q3 fiscal 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 | article 37\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 37,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-078\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2027-Q3\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin: **74%** | basis: company guided, down from fiscal Q2 2027 | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal Q3 2027 | said: not stored | article 115\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 115,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-079\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2027-Q3\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin guidance: **74%, plus or minus half a point** | basis: company guidance; management cited higher component costs, especially memory | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal Q3 2027 | said: not stored | article 232\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 232,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-080\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2027-Q3\",\n       \"line\": \"Gross margin guidance, low end: **73.5%** | basis: low end of management's gross margin guide for the quarter | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal Q3 2027 | said: not stored | article 177\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 177,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"On November 17, watch fiscal Q3 2027 gross margin against 73.5%, the low end of management’s gross margin guide for that quarter.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-081\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2027-Q3\",\n       \"line\": \"non-GAAP gross margin: **74%** | basis: non-GAAP | source as stored: NVIDIA Corp. | period as stored: fiscal Q3 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 | article 139\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 139,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA expects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, indicating continued sequential growth.\",\n          \"In comparison, NVIDIA expects a non-GAAP gross margin of 74% in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, highlighting its much stronger margin profile.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-082\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2027-Q3\",\n       \"line\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin guidance: **74%** | basis: Non-GAAP, company outlook | source as stored: NVIDIA Corporation | period as stored: fiscal third quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 | article 78\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 78,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA expects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, indicating continued sequential growth.\",\n          \"In comparison, NVIDIA expects a non-GAAP gross margin of 74% in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, highlighting its much stronger margin profile.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"b\",\n     \"value\": \"Gross margin 72% to 73% (as volumes scale)\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n     \"whose\": \"24/7 Wall St. (someone else's forecast section; source as stored: 24/7 Wall St.)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-125\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as someone else's forecast (estimate); the stored source is on each line > FY2028\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin: **72% to 73%** | basis: as volumes scale | source as stored: 24/7 Wall St. | period as stored: fiscal 2028 | said: not stored | article 46\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 46,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"b\",\n     \"value\": \"Gross margin under 72%, with recovery expected thereafter\",\n     \"period\": \"fourth quarter\",\n     \"whose\": \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg (someone else's forecast section; source as stored: Bloomberg (analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg))\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-132\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as someone else's forecast (estimate); the stored source is on each line > fourth quarter\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin: **under 72%** | basis: analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg; recovery expected thereafter | source as stored: Bloomberg (analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg) | period as stored: fourth quarter | said: not stored | article 16\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 16,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"b\",\n     \"value\": \"Gross margin 71% to 72% (expected to bottom on memory cost inflation)\",\n     \"period\": \"Q4\",\n     \"whose\": \"24/7 Wall St. (someone else's forecast section; source as stored: 24/7 Wall St.)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-141\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as someone else's forecast (estimate); the stored source is on each line > q4\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin: **71% to 72%** | basis: expected to bottom on memory cost inflation | source as stored: 24/7 Wall St. | period as stored: Q4 | said: not stored | article 46\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 46,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"c\",\n     \"value\": \"Gross margin 75% (GAAP per T-014's article 78; up from 72.4% a year ago)\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (also stored as 'fiscal second quarter of 2027', 'fiscal Q2 2027'); the same 75% is also stored under 'fiscal second quarter' (T-034), 'last quarter' (T-039), 'Reported quarter (not named in the item)' (T-058) and, as 'about 75%', with no period (T-075)\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA, reported (actual section; source as stored NVIDIA / NVIDIA Corp. / NVIDIA Corporation / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress); said 2026-08-26)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-014\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Gross margin: **75%** | stored from 8 articles, each with its own details: || article 73: basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026) | said: not stored || article 78: basis: GAAP gross margin; up from 72.4% a year ago | source as stored: NVIDIA Corporation | period as stored: fiscal second quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 || article 115: basis: company | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal Q2 2027 | said: not stored || article 121: basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) | period as stored: Fiscal 2027 second quarter | said: late August 2026 || article 122: basis: Up from 72.4% a year ago | source as stored: NVIDIA Corporation | period as stored: fiscal second quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 || article 139: basis: up from 72.4% a year ago | source as stored: NVIDIA Corp. | period as stored: fiscal Q2 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 || article 177: basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal Q2 2027 | said: August 2026 || article 232: basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal Q2 2027 | said: not stored\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 73,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\",\n          \"Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 78,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\",\n          \"Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 115,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 121,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 122,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.\",\n          \"Along with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s gross margin rose to 75% from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased. NVIDIA’s GAAP operating income has more than doubled year over year, implying significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 139,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 177,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs.\",\n          \"Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 232,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-034\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin: **75%** | basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal second quarter | said: not stored | article 55\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 55,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year.\",\n          \"The company also produced a 75% gross margin\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-039\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > last quarter\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin: **75%** | basis: company gross margin, as reported | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: last quarter | said: not stored | article 16\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 16,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-058\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > reported quarter (not named in the item)\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin: **75%** | basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA Corporation | period as stored: Reported quarter (not named in the item) | said: not stored | article 74\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 74,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) generated $96.2 billion of quarterly revenue, up 106%, with Data Center revenue rising 117% to $89.0 billion and gross margin at 75%.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-075\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > (no period stored)\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin: **about 75%** | basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: not stored | said: not stored | article 166\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 166,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"This is for a company with about 75% gross margins that grew its revenue by more than 100% last quarter and projected that it would increase its revenue by around 70% next fiscal year.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"c\",\n     \"value\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin 75% (versus 72.5% in the prior-year quarter)\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 ('Fiscal second quarter of 2027' in article 35)\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA, reported (actual section; source as stored NVIDIA; said 2026-08-26)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-016\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin: **75%** | stored from 2 articles, each with its own details: || article 35: basis: Non-GAAP; versus 72.5% in the prior-year quarter | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Fiscal second quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 || article 37: basis: non-GAAP; from 72.5% | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 | said: 2026-08-26\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 35,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 37,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n          \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\",\n          \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"c\",\n     \"value\": \"Gross margin change: 2.5 percentage points of expansion (GAAP/non-GAAP basis not specified)\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA, reported (actual section; source as stored NVIDIA)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-015\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"gross margin change: **2.5 percentage points** | basis: expansion as reported; GAAP/non-GAAP basis not specified | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 | said: not stored | article 151\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 151,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia reported exceptional financial results in the second quarter of fiscal 2027, which ended in July 2026. Revenue increased 106% to $96.2 billion, gross margin expanded 2.5 percentage points, and non-GAAP net income rose 120% to $2.22 per diluted share.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n }\n][\n {\n  \"article\": 16,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n  \"publisher\": \"qz.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia stock is approaching its cheapest valuation in a decade despite booming profits\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia stock is trading at less than 17 times forward earnings, half the multiple it commanded in 2025, even as revenue and net income are expected to surge\\nNvidia $NVDA stock is trading near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to Bloomberg , even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\\nAt under 17 times forward earnings, the stock's multiple has been cut in half compared with 2025 — a period when Nvidia 's expansion was actually slower — and has fallen sharply from above 25 times expected profits just this past May, according to Bloomberg.\\n\\\"The stock has de-rated pretty significantly, which suggests a healthy dose of skepticism that the company's current earnings power is sustainable,\\\" Eli Horton, a TCW senior portfolio manager overseeing thematic and durable growth equities, told Bloomberg. \\\"The stock's performance is surprising, given the backdrop of incredible fundamentals, but it tells you the market is expecting less than what the consensus is currently estimating.\\\"\\nOne drag on the stock's valuation is a squeeze on profitability. Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\\nCompetition is another concern. David Russell, global head of market strategy at TradeStation, pointed to the growing threat of customers building their own silicon, noting that Meta $META Platforms and Alphabet $GOOGL are among the major Nvidia buyers that have moved to design AI chips internally — a shift he believes will gradually erode Nvidia 's pricing power and market share. \\\"Multiples expand when companies are well positioned with potential to get better, and Nvidia doesn't offer that,\\\" Russell said.\\nNvidia stock has gained 22% so far in 2026, putting it second among the Magnificent Seven behind Apple $AAPL 's 25% advance — yet that performance looks modest against the broader chip sector. The Philadelphia Stock Exchange Semiconductor Index has surged nearly 76%, with Intel $INTC and Advanced Micro Devices both more than tripling; Nvidia sits near the bottom of the index's leaderboard, even as the index itself carries a valuation of 20 times forward earnings.\\nNvidia Chief Executive Officer Jensen Huang has pushed back on the market's assessment, calling the company \\\"the world's first and only growth value stock\\\" and describing Nvidia as \\\"incredibly misunderstood\\\" at a Goldman Sachs $GS technology conference earlier this month.\\nThe valuation discount follows a stretch of strong results. Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\\nHorton said the current multiple looks favorable given that neither a pullback in AI infrastructure spending nor a major regulatory intervention appears imminent. \\\"This seems like a very favorable multiple to have as an entry point,\\\" he said.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 35,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n  \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Sandisk vs. NVIDIA: Which AI Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\",\n  \"text\": \"Lately, surging artificial intelligence (AI) and data center demand has benefited both NVIDIA Corporation NVDA and the much smaller Sandisk Corporation SNDK. While the two companies offer different ways to capitalize on the AI boom, which one stands out to be the better buy now? Let's explore –\\nSandisk's AI-Driven Growth Boosts Revenues and Profitability\\nTotal revenues for Sandisk reached $8.97 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026, up a whopping 372% year over year and 51% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 5 press release.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, revenues reached $20.25 billion, representing 175% year-over-year growth. The Data Center segment has been the major growth driver, with revenues jumping 437% as AI and data center demand increased the need for high-value storage.\\nIn the fourth fiscal quarter, revenue growth came more from higher pricing than higher volumes. As a result, gross margins rose to 84.6% compared to 26.2% a year ago.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, gross margin was 71.5% compared to 30.1% a year earlier. Profitability also improved dramatically, with GAAP net income coming in at $11.43 billion versus a loss of $1.64 billion in the previous year.\\nThe growth doesn't seem to be temporary, as management remains bullish on their near-term outlook. They expect revenues to be between $10.3 billion and $10.8 billion in the fiscal first quarter of 2027, which would mean another strong quarterly growth following a huge fiscal fourth quarter. Now that the company has signed five more New Business Model agreements, revenue growth and profitability will surely improve.\\nNVIDIA's AI Demand Drives Strong Growth and Margin Expansion\\nThe Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\\nNVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago. Strong operating performance helped NVIDIA convert robust revenue growth into solid earnings growth.\\nNVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise. Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\\nBoth Sandisk and NVIDIA have delivered exceptional earnings growth, fueled by an increase in AI and data center demand, resulting in sharp revenue gains and substantial margin expansion. Both companies also look quite confident about their near-term outlooks.\\nHowever, Sandisk is in a highly cyclical memory business. If supply catches up with demand, memory prices may come under pressure and weigh on margins. Moreover, the company's fast-paced growth has raised expectations, which has made its growth trajectory susceptible if pricing momentum weakens.\\nSince Sandisk's business is dependent on storage demand, it has created greater concentration risk. In contrast, NVIDIA is capitalizing on the broader AI ecosystem, has dominant data center exposure, and continues to deliver exceptional AI-driven growth. To top it off, NVIDIA's 63.7% net profit margin compared with Sandisk's 56.5%, indicates its greater efficiency in converting revenues into bottom-line profits.\\nTaken together, these factors make NVIDIA a better buy than Sandisk at the moment. NVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Sandisk has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today's Zacks Rank #1 stocks here.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 73,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"title\": \"I'm Buying More Nvidia Stock Right Now. Here's Why.\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has grown into the world's largest company, with a market cap of more than $5 trillion. Its graphics processing units (GPUs) are the gold standard for training and running high-level computing and artificial intelligence programs.\\nSome people may, however, look at Nvidia's stock price and think that the shine is coming off Jensen Huang's company. Shares are up 21% so far this year, which is fine, but that's not even close to the 239% gain in 2023, the 171% jump in 2024, or even last year's 39% increase. Meanwhile, stocks like Sandisk , Micron , and Intel are all up more than 200% this year and have become Wall Street darlings.\\nAre Nvidia's best days in the rearview mirror? Absolutely not. While the chipmaker may not replicate its massive 2023 gain, Nvidia remains an incredibly profitable company whose growth story is far from over. In fact, I think Nvidia is going to be putting up some wild numbers in the next two or three years, and that's why I'll be buying more Nvidia shares .\\nNvidia is still growing fast, despite its size\\nWhat stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\\nNet income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\\n\\\"We're the only company in the world that creates and builds, offers an entire AI factory platform, a full-stack system. And customers can still mix and match,\\\" CEO Jensen Huang said. \\\"However, most companies just don't have the skills to do that or desire to do that. And so, there's an entire part of the market that we experience growth. There's sovereign AI, there're regional AIs, there're neoclouds, there're AI start-ups, there're enterprises, where we're seeing -- which represents about half of our business, and that's growing 100% a year.\\\"\\nNvidia's new product line is just getting started\\nNvidia's Hopper and Blackwell architectures have been enormously popular among hyperscalers and neoclouds building computing capacity to meet the growing demand for AI. But the company's Vera Rubin architecture, which uses NVLink chip-to-chip interconnects so its Rubin GPUs and Vera central processing units (CPUs) can share memory and improve efficiency, generates even more revenue. CFO Colette Kress said the Vera Rubin -- now in full production -- and full-stack AI platform has expanded the company's total addressable market from a range of $18 billion to $25 billion per gigawatt from Blackwell to $40 billion from Vera Rubin.\\nThat's one of the reasons why Huang has been so confident in his prediction -- which he made last year and recently reiterated -- that AI infrastructure spending would reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030. And speaking to reporters last week while in Scotland, Huang suggested that Nvidia would double its chip sales in the coming year.\\nIt's hard to get a group of people to agree on anything -- that's just human nature -- but Wall Street experts are nearly unanimous on Nvidia stock. Analysts at Rosenblatt have a \\\"Buy\\\" recommendation and a $390 price target, representing potential upside of 73%. Bernstein's analysts have a $400 price target and an \\\"Outperform\\\" rating, and Raymond James analysts have a whopping $515 price target and a \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating.\\nNvidia is expected to report another strong quarter when it next reports earnings (likely in late November), with the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year. And more of the same is expected for next year, following the Vera Rubin ramp-up.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 92,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n  \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n  \"title\": \"NVIDIA's $279B Supply Commitments: Can These Secure Its Growth Runway?\",\n  \"text\": \"NVIDIA Corporation 's NVDA increase in supply and capacity commitments signals confidence in sustained artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure demand. At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products. This gives NVIDIA greater visibility into component availability as it ramps up Blackwell and the next-generation Vera Rubin platform.\\nThe timing is important. NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion. The company expects third-quarter revenues of $108 billion (+/- 2%), indicating 89.5% year-over-year growth and a 12.2% sequential increase. Vera Rubin began production shipments in the third quarter.\\nThe supply commitments could help NVIDIA avoid shortages that might otherwise limit revenue growth. Securing memory and critical components in advance should support smoother production as AI customers expand data center deployments. NVIDIA also expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand.\\nHowever, the commitments create risks. NVIDIA could face higher costs or excess inventory if customer demand slows or product transitions occur faster than expected. The company warns that changing demand could make commitments difficult to reduce.\\nOverall, the $279 billion commitment provides NVIDIA with a stronger supply base for its AI growth plans, but execution and demand will determine how effectively that capacity converts into revenues. The Zacks Consensus Estimate for fiscal 2027 revenues is currently pegged at $406.05 billion, indicating an 88% year-over-year increase.\\nAMD and Intel Are Challenging NVIDIA in AI Race\\nAdvanced Micro Devices, Inc. AMD and Intel Corporation INTC are expanding their AI businesses.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices is broadening its data center AI opportunity from Instinct accelerators to the Helios rack-scale platform. In the second quarter of 2026, AMD's Data Center revenues rose 107% year over year to $6.7 billion, while Instinct sales more than doubled. Helios is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and 2027.\\nThe Helios platform has already garnered multiple large deals from big tech companies. Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of MI450-series GPUs using the AMD Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027. Microsoft plans to deploy Helios at scale on Azure. Advanced Micro Devices expects Data Center segment revenues to more than double year over year in 2027.\\nIntel is also gaining momentum in data center AI. Its second-quarter revenues rose 25% to $16.13 billion, while Data Center and AI revenues surged 59% to $6.26 billion. Intel also launched Xeon 6+ and expanded its open-source OpenVINO Physical AI framework, giving developers tools for AI and robotics applications.\\nIntel is building a broader AI infrastructure platform spanning CPUs, ASICs, graphics, networking and advanced packaging as customers design workload-specific systems. In the second quarter of 2026, purpose-built silicon revenues increased about 20% sequentially and nearly tripled year over year. During the last earnings call, management noted that purpose-built silicon revenues are approaching an estimated $2 billion run rate, and the company is targeting $4 billion in the near future.\\nShares of NVIDIA have risen around 20.8% year to date, underperforming the Zacks Computer and Technology sector's gain of 23.4%.\\nFrom a valuation standpoint, NVDA trades at a forward price-to-earnings ratio of 17.11, below the sector's average of 21.45.\\nThe Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 and 2028 earnings implies a year-over-year increase of approximately 93.9% and 65.8%, respectively. Estimates for fiscal 2027 and 2028 have been revised upward over the past 30 days.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 121,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\\n\\\"Now, compute is revenue,\\\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\\nWhen might it happen?\\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 166,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"title\": \"3 Reasons Why Nvidia Fits Warren Buffett's Investment Style\",\n  \"text\": \"While Warren Buffett and Berkshire Hathaway have never owned Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the chipmaker actually fits the investment profile of the stocks he loves to own. The semiconductor stock has a wide moat with a durable, compounding business model that is trading at an attractive valuation.\\nLet's dig deeper into why Nvidia should be considered a Buffett-style investment .\\nNvidia didn't stumble into becoming the king of artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure by accident. It was a carefully orchestrated plan that took many years to pay off, but it resulted in a wide moat in the space.\\nIt all started with its CUDA software platform , which the company created to let developers program its powerful graphics processing units (GPUs) for tasks outside of their original purpose of speeding up graphics rendering in video games. Nvidia then took CUDA and planted it into universities and research labs that were doing early work on AI. That resulted in an entire generation of AI engineers trained on its software platform and most foundational AI code written using its software and optimized for its chips.\\nThose moves formed the wide moat that Nvidia sees in AI infrastructure today, particularly with regard to large language model (LLM) training.\\nHowever, the company did not stop there, designing a whole AI infrastructure ecosystem around CUDA and its GPUs. It developed its proprietary NVLink interconnect technology to help its chips act as a single unit, further widening its moat, and in 2020 it acquired next-gen networking company Mellanox, whose technology was arguably ahead of its time. Earlier this year, Nvidia \\\"acquired\\\" Groq to bring its language processing unit (LPU) technology into the CUDA ecosystem, putting Nvidia at the forefront of the fast-growing inference market.\\nNvidia has also made other smart moves to make sure its moat remains intact. It locked in $500 billion worth of supply constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), which is needed to optimize AI chips, and it also acquired important open-source AI systems in SchedMD and HuggingFace to move into the critical software layers used to orchestrate and distribute AI.\\nIn the span of two decades, Nvidia has gone from a video game chipmaker to a complete end-to-end infrastructure player with a wide and perhaps growing moat.\\nWarren Buffett championed Berkshire's investment in Alphabet and gave the go-ahead to invest an additional $10 billion in a private placement to help the company build out the AI infrastructure for its cloud computing unit. As such, he clearly believes in cloud computing economics.\\nWhen using Nvidia chips, cloud providers have typically been getting paybacks within two to three years, while the chips tend to have economic life spans of five to six years. That gives Nvidia a visible replacement cycle that is pretty similar to Buffett's top holding, Apple , with its iPhones and computers.\\nMeanwhile, the great thing about AI versus past huge tech trends, like the internet and smartphones, is that its demand can essentially be limitless if it continues to provide a strong economic payback, not bound by human consumption. Combined with a well-defined replacement cycle, Nvidia should have a strong compounding business even when the AI frenzy starts to calm down.\\nEven with great businesses, Buffett isn't willing to pay any price. He wants to buy stocks when they are trading below their intrinsic value, and Nvidia can certainly make that argument.\\nThe stock trades at a forward P/E of 24 times fiscal 2027 (ending January 2027) and just above 14 times the fiscal 2028 consensus. This is for a company with about 75% gross margins that grew its revenue by more than 100% last quarter and projected that it would increase its revenue by around 70% next fiscal year.\\nWhile revenue growth will eventually slow, Nvidia is a stock worth a lot more than it is currently valued.\\nBy Geoffrey Seiler – Sep 27, 2026 at 3:55AM EST\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 222,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n  \"publisher\": \"gurufocus.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia Just Put a Record $150 Billion Behind Its Own Stock\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA , Financials ) is putting another $150 billion behind its own stock at a time when its valuation looks unusually low by historical standards.\\nThe chipmaker increased its share-repurchase authorization by a record $150 billion, lifting its remaining buyback capacity to $235 billion. Nvidia expects to use that authorization through fiscal 2028.\\nThe new authorization even tops Apple's $110 billion buyback approval in 2024.\\nWhat's more unusual is the timing.\\nNvidia trades at roughly 16.5 times 12-month forward earnings, its lowest multiple since January 2015 and well below its 15-year average of 30 times, according to LSEG data cited by Reuters.\\nThe lower multiple comes despite Nvidia forecasting roughly 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028 as demand for AI training and inference continues to drive its business.\\nCEO Jensen Huang said Nvidia's cash generation allows the company to keep investing in AI technology while returning capital to shareholders.\\nNvidia ended its July quarter with $22.44 billion in cash and cash equivalents. The company had previously announced an $80 billion buyback in May.\\nShares rose nearly 2% Monday and have gained more than 20% this year through Friday.\\nThe $235 billion authorization now gives Nvidia substantial room to repurchase shares through fiscal 2028, even as investors debate how long today's pace of AI spending can continue.\"\n }\n]","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/record-3/week-compare-packet-m03.json\\\")); p=p[0] if isinstance(p,list) else p; print(\\\"MATTER\\\",p[\\\"matter\\\"]); [print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",s,\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"\\\\n\\\".join(str((x.get(\\\"group\\\"),x.get(\\\"value\\\"),x.get(\\\"period\\\"),x.get(\\\"whose\\\"),[(l[\\\"id\\\"],[(a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"passages\\\"]) for a in l[\\\"stored_passages\\\"]]) for l in x[\\\"cited_lines\\\"]])) for x in rows)) for s,rows in p[\\\"sets\\\"].items()]' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/record-3/week-compare-packet-m03.json\\\")); p=p[0] if isinstance(p,list) else p; print(\\\"MATTER\\\",p[\\\"matter\\\"]); [print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",s,\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"\\\\n\\\".join(str((x.get(\\\"group\\\"),x.get(\\\"value\\\"),x.get(\\\"period\\\"),x.get(\\\"whose\\\"),[(l[\\\"id\\\"],[(a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"passages\\\"]) for a in l[\\\"stored_passages\\\"]]) for l in x[\\\"cited_lines\\\"]])) for x in rows)) for s,rows in p[\\\"sets\\\"].items()]' \"","aggregated_output":"MATTER M03\n\n S1 \n ('a', 'Gross margin 72% to 73% (company gross margin guidance, lowered on higher-than-expected memory costs; helped by price increases taking effect in fiscal Q1 2028)', 'Fiscal 2028', \"NVIDIA's own outlook (section: Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance); source as stored: NVIDIA management / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) / NVIDIA; said late August 2026 where stored)\", [('T-092', [(98, ['Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.']), (121, [\"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"]), (177, ['NVIDIA has already set price increases that take effect in fiscal Q1 2028. Management expects those higher prices to lift margin to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028.'])])])\n('a', 'Gross margin 71% to 72% (expected low point / trough, caused by higher memory prices)', 'fiscal Q4 2027 (stored as fiscal Q4 2027 / Fiscal 2027 fourth quarter)', \"NVIDIA's own outlook (section: Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance); source as stored: NVIDIA / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress); said August 2026 / late August 2026 where stored)\", [('T-090', [(115, ['Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.']), (177, ['In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs.', 'Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.'])]), ('T-091', [(121, [\"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"])])])\n('a', 'Gross margin approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points (GAAP and non-GAAP; low end 73.5%; down from fiscal Q2 2027, management cited higher component costs, especially memory)', 'fiscal Q3 2027 (stored as Q3 fiscal 2027 / fiscal Q3 2027 / fiscal third quarter of 2027)', \"NVIDIA's own outlook (section: Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance); source as stored: NVIDIA / NVIDIA Corp. / NVIDIA Corporation; said 2026-08-26 where stored)\", [('T-077', [(37, ['Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.'])]), ('T-078', [(115, ['Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.'])]), ('T-079', [(232, ['For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.'])]), ('T-080', [(177, ['On November 17, watch fiscal Q3 2027 gross margin against 73.5%, the low end of management’s gross margin guide for that quarter.'])]), ('T-081', [(139, ['NVIDIA expects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, indicating continued sequential growth.', 'In comparison, NVIDIA expects a non-GAAP gross margin of 74% in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, highlighting its much stronger margin profile.'])]), ('T-082', [(78, ['NVIDIA expects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, indicating continued sequential growth.', 'In comparison, NVIDIA expects a non-GAAP gross margin of 74% in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, highlighting its much stronger margin profile.'])])])\n('b', \"Gross margin 72% to 73% (as volumes scale; the same range the table stores as NVIDIA's own outlook in T-092)\", 'fiscal 2028', \"24/7 Wall St. (section: Stored as someone else's forecast (estimate); source as stored: 24/7 Wall St.)\", [('T-125', [(46, ['Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.'])])])\n('b', \"Gross margin 71% to 72% (expected to bottom on memory cost inflation; the same range the table stores as NVIDIA's own outlook for fiscal Q4 2027 in T-090, T-091)\", 'Q4', \"24/7 Wall St. (section: Stored as someone else's forecast (estimate); source as stored: 24/7 Wall St.)\", [('T-141', [(46, ['Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.'])])])\n('b', 'Gross margin under 72% (recovery expected thereafter)', 'fourth quarter', \"analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg (section: Stored as someone else's forecast (estimate); source as stored: Bloomberg (analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg))\", [('T-132', [(16, [\"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"])])])\n('c', \"Gross margin 75% (one article's basis says GAAP; up from 72.4% a year ago; T-015 stores an expansion of 2.5 percentage points, GAAP/non-GAAP basis not specified)\", \"fiscal Q2 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026) in T-014, T-015; the same 75% is also stored under 'fiscal second quarter' (T-034), 'last quarter' (T-039), 'Reported quarter (not named in the item)' (T-058) and with no period as 'about 75%' (T-075)\", 'NVIDIA, reported (section: Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual); source as stored: NVIDIA / NVIDIA Corporation / NVIDIA Corp. / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress))', [('T-014', [(73, [\"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\", 'Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.']), (78, [\"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\", 'Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.', \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"]), (115, ['Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.']), (121, [\"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"]), (122, ['NVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.', 'Along with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s gross margin rose to 75% from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased. NVIDIA’s GAAP operating income has more than doubled year over year, implying significant operating leverage.']), (139, [\"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\", \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"]), (177, ['In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs.', 'Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.']), (232, ['For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.'])]), ('T-015', [(151, ['Nvidia reported exceptional financial results in the second quarter of fiscal 2027, which ended in July 2026. Revenue increased 106% to $96.2 billion, gross margin expanded 2.5 percentage points, and non-GAAP net income rose 120% to $2.22 per diluted share.'])]), ('T-034', [(55, ['Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year.', 'The company also produced a 75% gross margin'])]), ('T-039', [(16, [\"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"])]), ('T-058', [(74, ['NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) generated $96.2 billion of quarterly revenue, up 106%, with Data Center revenue rising 117% to $89.0 billion and gross margin at 75%.'])]), ('T-075', [(166, ['This is for a company with about 75% gross margins that grew its revenue by more than 100% last quarter and projected that it would increase its revenue by around 70% next fiscal year.'])])])\n('c', 'Non-GAAP gross margin 75% (versus 72.5% in the prior-year quarter)', 'fiscal Q2 2027 (stored as Fiscal second quarter of 2027 / Q2 fiscal 2027)', 'NVIDIA, reported (section: Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual); source as stored: NVIDIA; said 2026-08-26)', [('T-016', [(35, [\"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\", \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"]), (37, [\"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\", \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\", \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"])])])\n\n S2 \n ('a', 'Gross margin 72% to 73%', 'Fiscal 2028; fiscal 2028', 'NVIDIA management / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) / NVIDIA, company outlook', [('T-092', [(98, ['Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.']), (121, [\"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"]), (177, ['NVIDIA has already set price increases that take effect in fiscal Q1 2028. Management expects those higher prices to lift margin to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028.'])])])\n('b', 'Gross margin 72% to 73%', 'fiscal 2028', '24/7 Wall St.', [('T-125', [(46, ['Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.'])])])\n('c', 'Gross margin 75%, including a GAAP figure; non-GAAP gross margin 75%', 'Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026); fiscal second quarter of 2027; fiscal Q2 2027; Fiscal 2027 second quarter; Fiscal second quarter of 2027; Q2 fiscal 2027', 'NVIDIA / NVIDIA Corporation / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) / NVIDIA Corp., reported', [('T-014', [(73, [\"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\", 'Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.']), (78, [\"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\", 'Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.', \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"]), (115, ['Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.']), (121, [\"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"]), (122, ['NVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.', 'Along with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s gross margin rose to 75% from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased. NVIDIA’s GAAP operating income has more than doubled year over year, implying significant operating leverage.']), (139, [\"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\", \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"]), (177, ['In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs.', 'Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.']), (232, ['For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.'])]), ('T-016', [(35, [\"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\", \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"]), (37, [\"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\", \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\", \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"])])])\n('c', 'Gross margin 75%', 'last quarter', 'NVIDIA, reported', [('T-039', [(16, [\"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"])])])\n\n S3 \n ('a', '72% to 73% gross margin', 'Fiscal 2028 / fiscal 2028', 'NVIDIA management; NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress); NVIDIA', [('T-092', [(98, ['Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.']), (121, [\"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"]), (177, ['NVIDIA has already set price increases that take effect in fiscal Q1 2028. Management expects those higher prices to lift margin to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028.'])])])\n('a', '71% to 72% gross margin, expected low point', 'fiscal Q4 2027 / Fiscal 2027 fourth quarter', 'NVIDIA; NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)', [('T-090', [(115, ['Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.']), (177, ['In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs.', 'Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.'])]), ('T-091', [(121, [\"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"])])])\n('b', '72% to 73% gross margin', 'fiscal 2028', '24/7 Wall St.', [('T-125', [(46, ['Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.'])])])\n('c', '75% gross margin; one stored basis specifies GAAP', 'Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026) / fiscal second quarter of 2027 / fiscal Q2 2027 / Fiscal 2027 second quarter', 'NVIDIA; NVIDIA Corporation; NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress); NVIDIA Corp., reported', [('T-014', [(73, [\"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\", 'Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.']), (78, [\"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\", 'Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.', \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"]), (115, ['Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.']), (121, [\"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"]), (122, ['NVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.', 'Along with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s gross margin rose to 75% from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased. NVIDIA’s GAAP operating income has more than doubled year over year, implying significant operating leverage.']), (139, [\"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\", \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"]), (177, ['In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs.', 'Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.']), (232, ['For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.'])])])\n('c', '75% non-GAAP gross margin', 'Fiscal second quarter of 2027 / Q2 fiscal 2027', 'NVIDIA, reported', [('T-016', [(35, [\"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\", \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"]), (37, [\"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\", \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\", \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"])])])\n('c', '75% gross margin', 'fiscal second quarter / last quarter / Reported quarter (not named in the item)', 'NVIDIA; NVIDIA Corporation, reported', [('T-034', [(55, ['Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year.', 'The company also produced a 75% gross margin'])]), ('T-039', [(16, [\"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"])]), ('T-058', [(74, ['NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) generated $96.2 billion of quarterly revenue, up 106%, with Data Center revenue rising 117% to $89.0 billion and gross margin at 75%.'])])])\n\n S4 \n ('a', 'Gross margin 72% to 73% (company guidance, lowered on higher-than-expected memory costs; helped by price increases taking effect in fiscal Q1 2028)', 'Fiscal 2028', 'NVIDIA, its own outlook (guidance section; source as stored NVIDIA management / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) / NVIDIA; said late August 2026)', [('T-092', [(98, ['Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.']), (121, [\"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"]), (177, ['NVIDIA has already set price increases that take effect in fiscal Q1 2028. Management expects those higher prices to lift margin to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028.'])])])\n('a', 'Gross margin 71% to 72% (management expects this to be the low point or trough, caused by higher memory prices)', \"fiscal Q4 2027 ('Fiscal 2027 fourth quarter' in T-091)\", 'NVIDIA, its own outlook (guidance section; source as stored NVIDIA / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress); said August 2026 / late August 2026)', [('T-090', [(115, ['Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.']), (177, ['In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs.', 'Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.'])]), ('T-091', [(121, [\"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"])])])\n('a', 'Gross margin guidance approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points (GAAP and non-GAAP; low end 73.5%; down from fiscal Q2 2027; management cited higher component costs, especially memory)', \"Q3 fiscal 2027 (also stored as 'fiscal Q3 2027', 'fiscal third quarter of 2027')\", 'NVIDIA, its own outlook (guidance section; source as stored NVIDIA / NVIDIA Corp. / NVIDIA Corporation; said 2026-08-26)', [('T-077', [(37, ['Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.'])]), ('T-078', [(115, ['Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.'])]), ('T-079', [(232, ['For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.'])]), ('T-080', [(177, ['On November 17, watch fiscal Q3 2027 gross margin against 73.5%, the low end of management’s gross margin guide for that quarter.'])]), ('T-081', [(139, ['NVIDIA expects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, indicating continued sequential growth.', 'In comparison, NVIDIA expects a non-GAAP gross margin of 74% in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, highlighting its much stronger margin profile.'])]), ('T-082', [(78, ['NVIDIA expects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, indicating continued sequential growth.', 'In comparison, NVIDIA expects a non-GAAP gross margin of 74% in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, highlighting its much stronger margin profile.'])])])\n('b', 'Gross margin 72% to 73% (as volumes scale)', 'fiscal 2028', \"24/7 Wall St. (someone else's forecast section; source as stored: 24/7 Wall St.)\", [('T-125', [(46, ['Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.'])])])\n('b', 'Gross margin under 72%, with recovery expected thereafter', 'fourth quarter', \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg (someone else's forecast section; source as stored: Bloomberg (analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg))\", [('T-132', [(16, [\"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"])])])\n('b', 'Gross margin 71% to 72% (expected to bottom on memory cost inflation)', 'Q4', \"24/7 Wall St. (someone else's forecast section; source as stored: 24/7 Wall St.)\", [('T-141', [(46, ['Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.'])])])\n('c', \"Gross margin 75% (GAAP per T-014's article 78; up from 72.4% a year ago)\", \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (also stored as 'fiscal second quarter of 2027', 'fiscal Q2 2027'); the same 75% is also stored under 'fiscal second quarter' (T-034), 'last quarter' (T-039), 'Reported quarter (not named in the item)' (T-058) and, as 'about 75%', with no period (T-075)\", 'NVIDIA, reported (actual section; source as stored NVIDIA / NVIDIA Corp. / NVIDIA Corporation / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress); said 2026-08-26)', [('T-014', [(73, [\"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\", 'Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.']), (78, [\"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\", 'Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.', \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"]), (115, ['Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.']), (121, [\"Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"]), (122, ['NVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.', 'Along with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s gross margin rose to 75% from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased. NVIDIA’s GAAP operating income has more than doubled year over year, implying significant operating leverage.']), (139, [\"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\", \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"]), (177, ['In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs.', 'Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.']), (232, ['For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.'])]), ('T-034', [(55, ['Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year.', 'The company also produced a 75% gross margin'])]), ('T-039', [(16, [\"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"])]), ('T-058', [(74, ['NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) generated $96.2 billion of quarterly revenue, up 106%, with Data Center revenue rising 117% to $89.0 billion and gross margin at 75%.'])]), ('T-075', [(166, ['This is for a company with about 75% gross margins that grew its revenue by more than 100% last quarter and projected that it would increase its revenue by around 70% next fiscal year.'])])])\n('c', 'Non-GAAP gross margin 75% (versus 72.5% in the prior-year quarter)', \"Q2 fiscal 2027 ('Fiscal second quarter of 2027' in article 35)\", 'NVIDIA, reported (actual section; source as stored NVIDIA; said 2026-08-26)', [('T-016', [(35, [\"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\", \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"]), (37, [\"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\", \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\", \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"])])])\n('c', 'Gross margin change: 2.5 percentage points of expansion (GAAP/non-GAAP basis not specified)', 'Q2 fiscal 2027', 'NVIDIA, reported (actual section; source as stored NVIDIA)', [('T-015', [(151, ['Nvidia reported exceptional financial results in the second quarter of fiscal 2027, which ended in July 2026. Revenue increased 106% to $96.2 billion, gross margin expanded 2.5 percentage points, and non-GAAP net income rose 120% to $2.22 per diluted share.'])])])\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
